{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2"],"primary":"c1","title":"Cartwright Act: ban on use or distribution of common pricing algorithms (AB 325)","requirement":"No person may use or distribute a common pricing algorithm as part of a contract, combination or conspiracy to restrain trade. No person may use or distribute one while coercing another person to set or adopt its recommended price or commercial term for the same or similar products or services in California.","key_value":"Unlawful to use or distribute a common pricing algorithm (any methodology, including software, used by two or more persons that uses competitor data to recommend or influence a price or commercial term) (a) as part of a restraint-of-trade conspiracy, or (b) while coercing others to adopt its recommended price or commercial term","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2026-01-01","end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 16729 (added by Stats. 2025, ch. 338 (AB 325)); see also Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code § 16756.1","coverage_conditions":"Applies to any person, other than the end consumer, who uses or distributes a common pricing algorithm for products or services in California. The law covers all industries and is not limited to rental housing, so it reaches residential rent-setting.","exemptions":"Only algorithms used by two or more persons that use competitor data are covered. Single-firm algorithms using only the business's own data fall outside the definition. A secondary source notes that the statute does not expressly exempt non-confidential competitor data.","coverage_from":"c1","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
